# Ebb — Cold Start

State lives in git and the work graph, so this works after any shutdown, reboot, or fresh clone. Nothing needs saving on the way out.

## Minimum viable fleet

Commander + one worker + one reviewer. Start there. It is a working loop, and it is what makes this installable rather than aspirational.

## 1. Commander

```bash
cd ~/code/ebb
claude
```

The folder's `CLAUDE.md` makes this session the commander. No launcher required.

> If you run your own agent harness, launch the commander with it instead — the folder context does the rest.

## 2. Seats

Each seat is a terminal you open and leave open — the session in it IS the seat. One terminal per seat, one account per seat. `wheelhouse/fleet/SEATS.md`'s "Launching a seat" is the full procedure, including the `claude` check, the first-run login, the standing prompt to paste as the first message, and what the seat does afterwards. The blocks below are that procedure with this project's root, seat root and seat names already filled in.

This project's seat root is `$HOME/.claude-seats-ebb` and its seat names are prefixed `ebb-` — namespace `ebb`, recorded as `namespace=ebb` in `wheelhouse/.template-source` and written out in `### Seat namespace` in `wheelhouse/fleet/SEATS.md`. That root is per-project because this machine may host another wheelhouse; the prefix is per-project because the commander's registry is shared even when the roots are not.

Each block ends with the check from the launch procedure: it prints the project roots of any sessions the directory already holds. Nothing, or `/Users/you/code/ebb`, is fine. Any other path is another fleet's seat — stop, do not run `claude` against it.

`--permission-mode auto` is what keeps a promoted seat from stalling on permission dialogs in a terminal nobody is watching — routine actions run without asking, reviewed by the harness's classifier instead of by you — and omitting the flag runs the seat stricter, prompting per the harness's defaults.

`ebb-worker-1`, on account `ebb-fleet-1`:

```bash
export CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/.claude-seats-ebb/ebb-worker-1"
if [ -d "$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/sessions" ]; then
  sed -n 's/.*"cwd":"\([^"]*\)".*/\1/p' "$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"/sessions/*.json 2>/dev/null | sort -u
fi
cd ~/code/ebb
claude --permission-mode auto
```

`ebb-reviewer`, on account `ebb-fleet-2`:

```bash
export CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR="$HOME/.claude-seats-ebb/ebb-reviewer"
if [ -d "$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/sessions" ]; then
  sed -n 's/.*"cwd":"\([^"]*\)".*/\1/p' "$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"/sessions/*.json 2>/dev/null | sort -u
fi
cd ~/code/ebb
claude --permission-mode auto
```

Then paste that seat's standing prompt from `wheelhouse/fleet/SEATS.md`, with `<seat-name>` replaced by the name above and only that seat's role brief kept. The seat idles until the commander sends it work by name.

## 3. Wire discovery, roll call, then verify

Per-seat config directories isolate each seat's session registry, so until the registrations are shared the commander cannot message a seat and no seat can reach the commander. You run the wiring, not the commander — an agent cannot write its own registry:

```bash
cd ~/code/ebb
wheelhouse/runbooks/wire-seats.sh --dry-run   # read-only: prints the plan
wheelhouse/runbooks/wire-seats.sh
```

Nothing to pass: the script reads `namespace=ebb` out of `wheelhouse/.template-source`, prints the root it settled on, and refuses the run if any seat in scope turns out to belong to another project on this machine.

Re-run it whenever a seat restarts. Then ask the commander for a roll call before it dispatches anything: wiring makes seats reachable but anonymous — registry rows carry derived names like `ebb-4f`, not seat names — and the roll call is what binds the map, then asserts no name in it resolves to two live rows. Finally have each seat message the commander unprompted, by name; a round trip only proves the commander can reach the seat. A seat that cannot answer gets relaunched, then re-wired and re-rolled. Full procedure and the reasoning: `wheelhouse/runbooks/SEAT_DISCOVERY.md`.

## 4. Feed it

Speak backlog items to the commander in plain language. It files them as beads, the fleet claims them, the reviewer gates them with bench evidence, and merges land per this project's merge policy.

## Shutdown

Close the terminals in any order. In-flight work resumes from the graph.
